1127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1397 0 R
/E (VADER works well on emoji-rich social media text -- it was designed for this, and the 91.9% accuracy on real TikTok comments confirmed the architecture choice made in D4. TF-IDF extracts keywords reliably but requires domain-specific stop words -- generic platform words \("content", "video"\) score highest and must be manually blocked.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1398 0 R
/E (Stripping emojis to "clean" the text hurt accuracy by 5.4 points -- the emoji is often the entire sentiment signal on TikTok. A 12-word GENERIC_FILLER list fixed what felt like a fundamental model problem -- the generic recommendations bug that drove the full 9-task refactor was a data filtering issue, not a model issue.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 1400 0 R
/T (Procedural)
/E (Procedural)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1401 0 R
/E (User testing surfaces problems developers cannot see -- testers immediately found the keyword cluster opacity and generic recommendations issues that code review missed. Building an end-to-end system takes longer than isolated modules -- connecting preprocessing to sentiment to recommendations introduced bugs that never appeared in unit testing.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1402 0 R
/E (One tester independently found the exact VADER hype slang failure that the D8 error analysis had predicted theoretically -- user testing confirmed a documented bug without being told to look for it. The hardest deliverable was evaluation \(D8\), not building \(D7\) -- measuring quality rigorously requires more discipline than writing the code.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1132 0 obj
<<
